8
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:45,010
Remember, just identify 3 trends to win.

9
00:00:45,970 --> 00:00:46,650
Are you ready?

10
00:00:48,070 --> 00:00:53,290
3, 2, 1, start!

11
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:30,600
Oh dear, why is it so challenging right from the start?

12
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:33,700
Is anyone struggling to figure it out?

13
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:38,860
Those who can identify all 3 trends in this sample within 2

14
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:45,920
minutes will receive a tap on the head from me, because this graph has no trend.

15
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:50,000
If it was a chart showing changes over time, it would cover

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,240
at least 2 years or more.

17
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:57,020
Otherwise, it's just a list of years on the x-axis.

18
00:01:57,470 --> 00:02:00,320
This chart belongs to a completely different type.

19
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:02,240
It's charts of comparison.

20
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:05,920
It doesn't have any trend, just comparison.

21
00:02:07,259 --> 00:02:09,240
Are you ready for the new chart type?

22
00:02:10,490 --> 00:02:11,600
Let's do this.

23
00:02:12,540 --> 00:02:22,250
In today's lesson, we will focus on 2 criteria, which are task

24
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:24,990
achievement and coherence and cohesion.

25
00:02:24,990 --> 00:02:29,670
With the V model, the method of thinking from general to specific

26
00:02:29,670 --> 00:02:35,830
and from specific to general, similar to charts with trends, we will

27
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:38,810
go through 3 steps of analysis.

28
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:44,610
The big 3, the great 3 and the major 3.

29
00:02:45,410 --> 00:02:47,130
Let's move on to the first step.

30
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:50,290
When you don't know where to start, please look at the big

31
00:02:50,290 --> 00:02:57,190
picture, the overview or what we call the big 3, then gradually narrow it down.

32
00:02:58,190 --> 00:03:02,950
The big 3 refer to 3 elements on the chart that immediately catch our eyes.

33
00:03:03,610 --> 00:03:07,930
The title of the chart, the y-axis and the x-axis.

34
00:03:08,610 --> 00:03:12,530
From the title of the chart, we can figure out what, here

35
00:03:12,530 --> 00:03:17,330
is the amount of tea and coffee imported, and where by 4

36
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:18,350
different countries.

37
00:03:19,230 --> 00:03:22,970
From the y-axis, we have the unit, which is tonnes.

38
00:03:23,670 --> 00:03:28,130
The x-axis is where the biggest difference lies, compared to charts

39
00:03:28,130 --> 00:03:32,170
with trends, instead of a time axis, here we see the names

40
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:38,810
of 4 countries, with each country having 2 data bars, corresponding to 2 imported items.

41
00:03:39,690 --> 00:03:43,370
We can consider this as a group of information or category.

42
00:03:44,270 --> 00:03:47,870
Please remember the name, so we can analyse the question more easily.

43
00:03:49,030 --> 00:03:54,230
Also, because the x-axis no longer provides information about years, let's

44
00:03:54,230 --> 00:03:59,330
pay closer attention to other details, such as the question or the

45
00:03:59,330 --> 00:04:02,610
chart, to see if the year is mentioned, so that we can

46
00:04:02,610 --> 00:04:04,230
identify the correct tense.

47
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:08,910
If we look at the chart and title, we see no specific

48
00:04:08,910 --> 00:04:13,050
year mentioned, so we'll use the present simple tense in this sample.

49
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:19,070
Please write a big present simple right below the chart title to remember.

50
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:23,770
To sum up, the big 3 provided us with the following information.

51
00:04:41,070 --> 00:04:44,950
Canada, the US, the UK and Germany.

52
00:04:49,500 --> 00:04:54,220
Next is the great 3, including 1.

53
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,440
Subjects of comparison 2.

54
00:04:57,880 --> 00:05:00,920
Global feature number 1 and 3.

55
00:05:01,380 --> 00:05:03,020
Global feature number 2.

56
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:04,460
Attention!

57
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:10,660
This is a chart of comparison, it means there are no trends

58
00:05:10,660 --> 00:05:15,180
at all, so do not write the global trend in the overview.

59
00:05:15,820 --> 00:05:21,180
Instead, we'll need a second global feature to have a complete overview,

60
00:05:21,420 --> 00:05:25,400
which will help you improve the task achievement score.

61
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:28,960
Firstly, subjects of comparison.

62
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:33,660
This is a bar chart, so the subjects of comparison will correspond

63
00:05:33,660 --> 00:05:37,480
to the subjects that are represented by two different colours in the

64
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:40,220
chart, which are tea and coffee.

65
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:43,020
Let's take an overview of this chart.

66
00:05:43,500 --> 00:05:45,420
What are the global features that you see?

67
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:50,480
Let me remind you, global features should focus on 1.

68
00:05:51,350 --> 00:05:56,220
Comparison – comparing factors such as the highest or lowest subject, or

69
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:58,420
which group is higher compared to others.

70
00:05:59,220 --> 00:05:59,740
2.

71
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:06,800
Special points – such as highest, lowest, starting, ending, intersecting points.

72
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:12,020
So, are there any comparisons or special points that catch your eye right away?

73
00:06:12,980 --> 00:06:16,520
It can be seen that the UK records the highest figure for

74
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:20,060
tea, while Germany reports the largest level for coffee.

75
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:25,760
Therefore, the first global feature is, the tea import is highest in

76
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:28,780
the UK, while coffee is highest in Germany.

77
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:33,480
And what about the variation, or in other words, the difference in

78
00:06:33,480 --> 00:06:35,920
the import amount between the given countries?

79
00:06:36,780 --> 00:06:42,600
It's obvious that the tea levels vary significantly, jumping from very low to very high.

80
00:06:43,380 --> 00:06:47,540
However, the coffee levels are much more consistent across the countries.

81
00:06:48,620 --> 00:06:53,640
Therefore, the second global feature is, the variation in tea import is

82
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:54,960
larger than in coffee.

83
00:06:55,860 --> 00:06:58,800
Let's take a look at the big three and the great three

84
00:06:58,800 --> 00:06:59,580
once again.

85
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:13,710
The last step in the analysis, the major three, goes into the

86
00:07:13,710 --> 00:07:18,930
details, looking at how information is grouped, how paragraphs are divided,

87
00:07:19,570 --> 00:07:24,210
selecting the most important individual features and arranging them in a

88
00:07:24,210 --> 00:07:26,990
logical way to create a complete writing.

89
00:07:27,690 --> 00:07:31,810
Let's remind a bit about the three elements of the major three.

90
00:07:32,690 --> 00:07:33,290
1.

91
00:07:33,850 --> 00:07:35,150
Grouping information.

92
00:07:35,690 --> 00:07:40,070
How to group information, dividing the body into two paragraphs.

93
00:07:41,590 --> 00:07:41,750
2.

94
00:07:42,270 --> 00:07:43,650
Individual features.

95
00:07:44,590 --> 00:07:48,690
Please note that this is a chart of comparison, so there's no

96
00:07:48,690 --> 00:07:53,030
trends, no individual trends, only individual features.

97
00:07:53,850 --> 00:07:54,170
3.

98
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:56,270
Detail organisation.

99
00:07:57,390 --> 00:08:00,030
Organise the information to describe the chart.

100
00:08:00,590 --> 00:08:05,690
This symbol, I'm sure you still remember it, when dividing paragraphs in

101
00:08:05,690 --> 00:08:10,590
Charts with Trends writing, we can use one of the two methods.

102
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:16,470
The vertical approach, dividing the chart from top to bottom, or by year.

103
00:08:17,010 --> 00:08:22,710
And the horizontal approach, dividing the chart from left to right, or

104
00:08:22,710 --> 00:08:25,070
grouping by subjects of comparison.

105
00:08:25,710 --> 00:08:30,150
For Charts of Comparison, since there are no trends, the way we

106
00:08:30,150 --> 00:08:32,809
divide paragraphs is slightly different.

107
00:08:33,370 --> 00:08:39,110
To effectively group the information, first, we need to identify what are

108
00:08:39,110 --> 00:08:40,150
being compared here.

109
00:08:40,789 --> 00:08:45,870
We can see that there are at least two comparisons to be made.

110
00:08:46,750 --> 00:08:47,190
1.

111
00:08:47,510 --> 00:08:49,370
Comparisons between categories.

112
00:08:50,310 --> 00:08:51,350
And 2.

113
00:08:51,810 --> 00:08:54,890
Comparisons between subjects of comparison.

114
00:08:55,810 --> 00:09:01,570
Correspondingly, we also have two methods of dividing the body for Charts of Comparison.

115
00:09:02,290 --> 00:09:02,750
1.

116
00:09:03,170 --> 00:09:04,350
Grouping by category.

117
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:05,950
And 2.

118
00:09:06,530 --> 00:09:08,490
Grouping by subject of comparison.

119
00:09:09,110 --> 00:09:14,110
Follow method 1, we have four categories corresponding to four countries.

120
00:09:14,970 --> 00:09:18,830
The easiest way to group them is to find which categories share

121
00:09:18,830 --> 00:09:24,710
similarities in terms of pattern, which means order of information, or in

122
00:09:24,710 --> 00:09:26,570
terms of data in those categories.

123
00:09:27,790 --> 00:09:32,410
Among the four countries, we see that Canada, the US and Germany

124
00:09:32,410 --> 00:09:34,010
follow a similar pattern.

125
00:09:34,950 --> 00:09:38,590
They all have higher figures for coffee imports compared to tea.

126
00:09:39,270 --> 00:09:42,750
So we could group these three countries into body 1.

127
00:09:43,410 --> 00:09:48,690
And for the UK, which shows the opposite trend, importing significantly more

128
00:09:48,690 --> 00:09:51,270
tea, let's put it into body 2.

129
00:09:52,110 --> 00:09:56,550
Sometimes we should follow method 2, grouping by subject of comparison.

130
00:09:57,630 --> 00:10:03,310
For example, in this chart we are dealing with two distinct commodities, tea and coffee.

131
00:10:04,450 --> 00:10:08,850
Comparing the country's performance for one product at a time is very logical.

132
00:10:08,850 --> 00:10:14,270
So, let's put all the data regarding tea import into one paragraph

133
00:10:14,270 --> 00:10:16,710
and coffee import into the other.

134
00:10:17,630 --> 00:10:22,110
This way we can clearly see which country ranks highest or lowest

135
00:10:22,110 --> 00:10:23,750
for each specific drink.

136
00:10:24,570 --> 00:10:29,030
Generally, both methods are effective, depending on each specific chart.

137
00:10:30,010 --> 00:10:32,810
You should apply whichever method seems easier for you.

138
00:10:33,330 --> 00:10:37,150
There is often more than one effective way to divide body paragraphs,

139
00:10:37,390 --> 00:10:41,270
so you can be confident in your choices and don't worry too much.

140
00:10:42,070 --> 00:10:46,730
For this essay, we will follow method 2, grouping by subject, as

141
00:10:46,730 --> 00:10:49,290
it allows for direct ranking of the countries.

142
00:10:50,150 --> 00:10:52,050
Body 1, tea import.

143
00:10:52,450 --> 00:10:54,510
Body 2, coffee import.

144
00:10:55,550 --> 00:11:00,050
Moving on to the second element in the major three, individual features,

145
00:11:00,550 --> 00:11:06,010
including special points, comparisons and numbers that need to be described in the writing.

146
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:08,730
Let's find out one by one.

147
00:11:09,510 --> 00:11:11,830
Firstly, let's look at tea import.

148
00:11:12,610 --> 00:11:17,010
The UK records the highest figure, reaching 80,000 tonnes.

149
00:11:17,970 --> 00:11:21,790
This is the most significant feature, and you should describe it in

150
00:11:21,790 --> 00:11:25,270
your writing because it is the only country where the amount of

151
00:11:25,270 --> 00:11:27,650
tea imported is higher than coffee.

152
00:11:28,750 --> 00:11:31,330
Germany follows with 40,000 tonnes.

153
00:11:32,330 --> 00:11:36,130
Note that this is exactly half of the UK's level, which is

154
00:11:36,130 --> 00:11:38,530
a great comparison to make in your report.

155
00:11:39,670 --> 00:11:43,550
Lastly, the US and Canada stand at the lowest positions.

156
00:11:44,550 --> 00:11:45,020
Their data is rather similar, with both countries importing approximately 20,000 tonnes.

157
00:11:51,270 --> 00:11:54,530
Moving on to the second subject, coffee import.

158
00:11:55,310 --> 00:12:01,130
First, Germany imports more coffee than any other country, with 50,000 tonnes.

159
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:05,810
This is the highest figure for coffee, making Germany the leader in

160
00:12:05,810 --> 00:12:06,430
this category.

161
00:12:07,710 --> 00:12:10,210
Next, we have Canada and the US.

162
00:12:10,810 --> 00:12:14,250
Their levels are neck and neck at around 40,000 tonnes.

163
00:12:15,090 --> 00:12:18,730
It is also notable that in these two countries, the amount of

164
00:12:18,730 --> 00:12:23,290
imported coffee is twice that for tea, a key comparison to mention.

165
00:12:24,730 --> 00:12:30,570
Finally, the United Kingdom ranks last in coffee import, with roughly 30,000 tonnes.

166
00:12:31,370 --> 00:12:35,210
This is a sharp contrast to its tea imports, as this figure

167
00:12:35,210 --> 00:12:38,390
is only about one third of the UK's level for tea.

168
00:12:38,810 --> 00:12:41,070
We've nearly finished the question analysis.

169
00:12:42,010 --> 00:12:45,310
The most important information has been selected.

170
00:12:46,230 --> 00:12:51,230
Now there's just one final step, detail organisation.

171
00:12:52,170 --> 00:12:56,790
It's about arranging the information in each paragraph in a logical way,

172
00:12:57,250 --> 00:12:59,910
so that we can have a complete outline.

173
00:13:00,770 --> 00:13:07,550
Remember, we have two paragraphing methods, grouping by category and grouping

174
00:13:07,550 --> 00:13:09,310
by subject of comparison.

175
00:13:10,170 --> 00:13:14,450
In each paragraph, the rule is to start from the most significant,

176
00:13:14,990 --> 00:13:20,870
the most important information, to the least significant, the least important one.

177
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:28,320
Hopefully, now you understand how to do it and not feel overwhelmed

178
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:30,560
by the information in this type anymore.

179
00:13:31,420 --> 00:13:36,000
With such a detailed analysis, you will achieve a band 6 plus

180
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,900
for task achievement and coherence and cohesion.

181
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:44,000
Using language carefully can lead to even higher scores.

182
00:13:44,760 --> 00:13:46,520
Stay confident, you guys!

183
00:13:50,830 --> 00:13:55,130
A challenging type of chart in task 1 has been handled.

184
00:13:55,850 --> 00:13:57,030
It's so great!

185
00:13:57,830 --> 00:14:00,890
Let me summarise a bit of the knowledge we've covered.

186
00:14:01,530 --> 00:14:07,850
We still analysed charts of comparison through three steps, similar to charts with trends.

187
00:14:07,850 --> 00:14:11,450
The big three, the great three, the major three.

188
00:14:12,410 --> 00:14:17,770
In the big three, as there's no time axis, we identify categories

189
00:14:17,770 --> 00:14:20,650
or groups of information instead of time.

190
00:14:21,270 --> 00:14:22,570
So here's what we have.

191
00:14:23,690 --> 00:14:29,710
1, what and where, 2, unit and 3, category.

192
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:36,270
The great three also has a small change, with global trend being

193
00:14:36,270 --> 00:14:41,430
replaced by the second global feature, along with subjects of comparison.

194
00:14:42,410 --> 00:14:47,030
Finally, the most significant changes lie within the major three.

195
00:14:48,170 --> 00:14:53,850
1, we don't group information by dividing the chart vertically or horizontally,

196
00:14:54,510 --> 00:15:01,770
like charts with trends, but grouping by category, grouping by subject of

197
00:15:01,770 --> 00:15:10,990
comparison, 2, no more individual trends, only individual features, and 3,

198
00:15:11,690 --> 00:15:17,110
lastly, it's still about organising the information to make a complete outline.

